Over the course of a decade, the Batavia was salvaged. Divers found apothecary jars, cannons, grenades, an anchor, and coins from the chests that were left behind. Between 1972 and 1976, work carried out by the Department of Maritime Archaeology led to parts of the ship's hull being discovered, then carefully recorded and erased. These were treated, restored, and then rebuilt, and are currently on display at the Shipwrecks Museum in the Western Australian city of Freemantle. The Batavia expedition also features various recovered artifacts, as well as a victim's skeleton. A replica of the doomed vessel was constructed in the 1990s, and is currently in Lelystot, a city in the central Netherlands.
